Fintech in Mauritius has grown rapidly over the last few years, especially in digital payments. High smartphone use and strong mobile networks such as 4G and 5G (DataReportal, 2025). make mobile apps part of our daily life from transfers to bill payments. Due to the pandemic period, businesses and consumers were pushed towards contactless payments. However, not everyone is switching to online payments at the same pace, as cash remains common among older people.

Blink faces strong competition from bank-led apps. Juice by MCB is the strongest competitor. It combines banking features with payment services, such as instant transfers and QR codes. It also serves hundreds of thousands of users and millions of transactions monthly (MCB, 2024). Other banks, such as the SBM and Absa also integrate payment options into their apps, so customers often pay directly without needing a separate wallet.

On the telecom side, there is direct competition between Blink and My.t money by Mauritius Telecom. Telecom operators have an advantage in digital payment since they already have wide mobile network coverage and a large number of subscribers (DataReportal, 2025). Both wallets use SIM access, agent networks, and mobile setup to attract customers with even little banking involvement. Additionally, they are commonly used for airtime purchase, small transfers or certain merchant payments. As a result, Blink operates mainly as a mobile wallet, while bank apps serve as all-in-one apps. This difference affects customer loyalty and growth of transactions over time.

The Bank of Mauritius has been central to modernising payments. Beyond setting rules, it also encourages electronic systems, interoperability, and safer apps. FinTech adoption is being supported by advances such as QR payment, faster transfer networks, and online identity verification like e-KYC (World Bank, 2021; CPMI/BIS, 2020; GSMA, 2023).For example, today more supermarkets, fuel stations, or small retailers accept QR or app payments and MCB reported a sharp increase in recent years (MCB, 2024). In addition, the government going digital in terms of tax and bill payments further encourages people to get used to online transactions. 

Mobile wallets like Blink remain important for including more people in the financial system. Even though most Mauritians already have bank accounts (World Bank, 2021), small vendors and younger users still find it difficult to use traditional banks and prefer simpler banking solutions. Blink makes it easier for them by allowing small payments without POS machines. These steps make daily transactions easier while also adding more people to the digital economy.

Despite these positives, the growth of fintech faces limitations. Mauritius has only about 1.3 million people (World Bank, 2024), which limits the number of transactions and potential revenues for fintech platforms. A few strong brands dominate, making it more difficult for new entrants to compete as they would have to spend a lot more to win customers. Some users are not comfortable with technology, especially when many fraud cases occur, they may lose trust. As a result, many users keep digital wallets as backup tools instead of their main way to pay. This is slowing the transition to digital payments.

